1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,120
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, where each week

2
00:00:03,173 --> 00:00:05,761
we explore the Torah portion through the eyes

3
00:00:05,814 --> 00:00:08,801
of the great medieval commentator, Rashi.

4
00:00:09,009 --> 00:00:12,961
This week's portion is called Re'eh, which means

5
00:00:13,041 --> 00:00:16,081
"Behold" or "See," and it's found in the

6
00:00:16,113 --> 00:00:19,761
book of Deuteronomy, chapters eleven through sixteen.

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,121
Let's dive in and see what Rashi reveals

8
00:00:23,161 --> 00:00:28,267
about this rich and complex portion.Re'eh is one

9
00:00:28,320 --> 00:00:30,667
of those portions that covers an enormous amount

10
00:00:30,694 --> 00:00:31,467
of ground.

11
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:34,427
Moses is speaking to the Israelites as they

12
00:00:34,507 --> 00:00:37,307
stand on the edge of the Promised Land,

13
00:00:37,327 --> 00:00:39,947
and he lays before them a dramatic choice:

14
00:00:40,071 --> 00:00:42,108
blessing and curse.

15
00:00:42,507 --> 00:00:46,827
If they follow God's commandments, blessing; if they

16
00:00:46,875 --> 00:00:49,387
turn away, curse.

17
00:00:49,531 --> 00:00:52,907
From there, the portion moves into detailed laws

18
00:00:52,960 --> 00:00:57,147
about centralizing worship in one chosen place, destroying

19
00:00:57,211 --> 00:01:01,387
idolatry, the rules around eating meat, tithes and

20
00:01:01,443 --> 00:01:04,987
offerings, the false prophet, the person who tries

21
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:08,107
to entice you to worship other gods, and

22
00:01:08,167 --> 00:01:09,147
the festivals.

23
00:01:09,547 --> 00:01:12,747
It's really a blueprint for how the Israelites

24
00:01:12,767 --> 00:01:14,987
are to live once they enter the land.

25
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:19,067
Rashi, as always, is drawn to the small

26
00:01:19,147 --> 00:01:21,467
details that reveal big ideas.

27
00:01:21,787 --> 00:01:26,587
He notices unusual word choices, doubled verbs, and

28
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:30,827
apparent contradictions between verses, and he uses these

29
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,227
as doorways into deeper meaning.

30
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:37,627
In this portion especially, Rashi pays close attention

31
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:40,910
to how the Torah distinguishes between different stages

32
00:01:40,963 --> 00:01:44,990
of Israel's history, between what's permitted now versus

33
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:50,524
later, and between the ideal and the practical.Let's

34
00:01:50,577 --> 00:01:52,844
start with a comment that seems simple but

35
00:01:52,904 --> 00:01:54,524
carries enormous weight.

36
00:01:54,791 --> 00:01:58,365
In chapter twelve, verse two, the Torah commands

37
00:01:58,385 --> 00:02:01,325
the Israelites to utterly destroy all the places

38
00:02:01,365 --> 00:02:03,565
where the nations served their gods.

39
00:02:03,832 --> 00:02:07,485
In Hebrew, the verse uses a doubled verb:

40
00:02:07,565 --> 00:02:12,205
aved te'abdun, which literally means "destroy, you shall

41
00:02:12,285 --> 00:02:16,125
destroy." Rashi seizes on this doubling and says

42
00:02:16,152 --> 00:02:19,565
it means: destroy and then destroy again.

43
00:02:19,858 --> 00:02:23,565
In other words, when uprooting idolatry, you must

44
00:02:23,618 --> 00:02:25,245
be absolutely thorough.

45
00:02:25,425 --> 00:02:27,725
You can't just knock down the statue and

46
00:02:27,789 --> 00:02:28,605
walk away.

47
00:02:28,705 --> 00:02:31,325
You have to remove every last trace.

48
00:02:31,517 --> 00:02:34,205
This isn't just a practical instruction.

49
00:02:34,365 --> 00:02:37,165
It's Rashi teaching us something about the nature

50
00:02:37,192 --> 00:02:40,765
of spiritual danger: half-measures aren't enough when it

51
00:02:40,805 --> 00:02:42,525
comes to things that can pull you away

52
00:02:42,573 --> 00:02:46,181
from your core values.Next, let's look at a

53
00:02:46,241 --> 00:02:49,621
fascinating comment about the laws of slaughtering animals

54
00:02:49,661 --> 00:02:50,581
for food.

55
00:02:50,794 --> 00:02:54,822
In chapter twelve, verse twenty-one, the Torah says:

56
00:02:55,022 --> 00:02:57,702
"Then thou shalt slaughter as I have commanded

57
00:02:57,766 --> 00:03:01,462
thee." Rashi picks up on those words, "as

58
00:03:01,542 --> 00:03:04,342
I have commanded thee," and asks a pointed

59
00:03:04,404 --> 00:03:05,142
question.

60
00:03:05,249 --> 00:03:08,582
Where exactly did God command the specific rules

61
00:03:08,635 --> 00:03:10,502
of how to slaughter an animal?

62
00:03:10,742 --> 00:03:13,865
If you search the entire written Torah, you

63
00:03:13,905 --> 00:03:17,865
won't find detailed instructions for ritual slaughter anywhere.

64
00:03:18,105 --> 00:03:21,865
Rashi's answer is striking: this verse is proof

65
00:03:21,897 --> 00:03:24,985
that there was an oral tradition, laws communicated

66
00:03:25,012 --> 00:03:27,305
to Moses on Mount Sinai that were never

67
00:03:27,345 --> 00:03:29,627
written down in the Torah itself.

68
00:03:29,755 --> 00:03:32,347
This is one of the most important comments

69
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:35,227
in the entire portion because it establishes a

70
00:03:35,307 --> 00:03:38,107
textual basis for the entire concept of the

71
00:03:38,203 --> 00:03:41,148
Oral Torah, the vast body of Jewish law

72
00:03:41,188 --> 00:03:44,028
and tradition that accompanies the written text.

73
00:03:44,268 --> 00:03:48,348
Rashi finds this enormous principle hiding inside just

74
00:03:48,428 --> 00:03:52,821
three Hebrew words.Now, here's a comment that touches

75
00:03:52,848 --> 00:03:54,421
on something very human.

76
00:03:54,714 --> 00:03:57,943
In chapter twelve, verse twenty, the Torah says

77
00:03:57,975 --> 00:04:00,743
that when God enlarges your territory, then you

78
00:04:00,803 --> 00:04:04,583
may say, "I will eat meat." Rashi draws

79
00:04:04,615 --> 00:04:07,863
from this a remarkable lesson about proper conduct.

80
00:04:08,103 --> 00:04:11,226
He says the Torah is teaching us derech

81
00:04:11,333 --> 00:04:14,586
eretz, the proper way to live: a person

82
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:17,146
should not desire to eat meat unless they

83
00:04:17,210 --> 00:04:19,706
live in a state of abundance and wealth.

84
00:04:20,106 --> 00:04:23,076
In other words, the Torah is offering guidance

85
00:04:23,136 --> 00:04:25,716
not just about what's permitted, but about how

86
00:04:25,769 --> 00:04:26,996
to manage desire.

87
00:04:27,256 --> 00:04:30,196
Don't reach for luxuries beyond your means.

88
00:04:30,463 --> 00:04:34,196
It's a surprisingly modern-sounding piece of financial and

89
00:04:34,256 --> 00:04:37,156
ethical wisdom, and Rashi draws it straight from

90
00:04:37,176 --> 00:04:40,497
the sequence of the verses.Let's turn to something

91
00:04:40,588 --> 00:04:42,817
darker but deeply important.

92
00:04:42,927 --> 00:04:46,097
Chapter thirteen discusses the case of a false

93
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:48,737
prophet who performs a sign or a wonder

94
00:04:48,817 --> 00:04:51,537
and then says, "Let us go worship other

95
00:04:51,649 --> 00:04:55,137
gods." The sign actually comes true, yet the

96
00:04:55,217 --> 00:04:57,779
Torah says: do not listen to him.

97
00:04:58,219 --> 00:05:01,459
A listener might reasonably ask, well, if the

98
00:05:01,523 --> 00:05:04,179
sign came true, doesn't that prove something?

99
00:05:04,472 --> 00:05:06,819
Rashi addresses this directly.

100
00:05:07,032 --> 00:05:09,619
He says that God allows the false prophet

101
00:05:09,672 --> 00:05:12,019
to perform the sign as a test: "For

102
00:05:12,039 --> 00:05:14,419
the Lord your God is testing you, to

103
00:05:14,467 --> 00:05:16,659
know whether you truly love the Lord your

104
00:05:16,779 --> 00:05:19,459
God." This is a profound idea.

105
00:05:19,606 --> 00:05:22,259
Truth isn't determined by spectacle.

106
00:05:22,539 --> 00:05:24,979
A miracle doesn't validate a message if the

107
00:05:25,029 --> 00:05:27,619
message itself contradicts what you know to be

108
00:05:27,712 --> 00:05:28,259
right.

109
00:05:28,579 --> 00:05:31,619
Rashi is teaching us that loyalty to core

110
00:05:31,684 --> 00:05:34,499
principles must be stronger than the dazzle of

111
00:05:34,562 --> 00:05:39,851
extraordinary events.Finally, consider Rashi's comment on the person

112
00:05:39,911 --> 00:05:42,891
who secretly entices you to worship idols.

113
00:05:43,091 --> 00:05:46,091
The Torah lists, in chapter thirteen, all the

114
00:05:46,148 --> 00:05:49,291
people closest to you who might try this:

115
00:05:49,339 --> 00:05:52,731
your brother, your mother's son, the wife of

116
00:05:52,763 --> 00:05:55,291
your bosom, your friend who is as your

117
00:05:55,371 --> 00:05:56,171
own soul.

118
00:05:56,518 --> 00:06:00,011
Rashi identifies "your friend who is as your

119
00:06:00,091 --> 00:06:02,651
own soul" as referring to your father.

120
00:06:02,918 --> 00:06:06,571
He then explains that Scripture deliberately lists those

121
00:06:06,591 --> 00:06:08,411
who are dearest to you to make the

122
00:06:08,478 --> 00:06:12,651
point unmistakable: if even these beloved people must

123
00:06:12,711 --> 00:06:14,571
not be spared when they try to pull

124
00:06:14,631 --> 00:06:17,291
you away from God, how much more so

125
00:06:17,331 --> 00:06:18,251
a stranger.

126
00:06:18,531 --> 00:06:21,375
And Rashi notes that the Torah says this

127
00:06:21,435 --> 00:06:25,375
enticement happens "in secret," because that's how seduction

128
00:06:25,423 --> 00:06:29,535
typically works, in whispers, in private, in the

129
00:06:29,606 --> 00:06:30,335
twilight.

130
00:06:30,535 --> 00:06:34,015
There's a deep psychological realism here.

131
00:06:34,362 --> 00:06:38,335
Rashi understands that the greatest spiritual threats often

132
00:06:38,375 --> 00:06:40,735
don't come from enemies but from those we

133
00:06:40,831 --> 00:06:44,335
love and trust, speaking quietly in moments of

134
00:06:44,398 --> 00:06:49,073
vulnerability.When we step back and look at Rashi's

135
00:06:49,124 --> 00:06:52,833
commentary across this entire portion, a few powerful

136
00:06:52,902 --> 00:06:54,193
themes emerge.

137
00:06:54,353 --> 00:06:58,433
First, Rashi is deeply concerned with thoroughness and

138
00:06:58,505 --> 00:06:59,473
precision.

139
00:06:59,553 --> 00:07:04,193
Whether it's uprooting idolatry completely, slaughtering animals according

140
00:07:04,246 --> 00:07:08,353
to exact oral traditions, or understanding exactly which

141
00:07:08,433 --> 00:07:13,393
sacrifices belong where, Rashi insists that details matter

142
00:07:13,513 --> 00:07:16,513
and that carelessness in spiritual life has real

143
00:07:16,587 --> 00:07:17,793
consequences.

144
00:07:17,976 --> 00:07:23,393
Second, Rashi consistently reveals layers beneath the surface.

145
00:07:23,553 --> 00:07:26,673
A doubled verb becomes a legal principle.

146
00:07:26,766 --> 00:07:29,873
Three words become proof of an entire oral

147
00:07:29,929 --> 00:07:30,753
tradition.

148
00:07:30,953 --> 00:07:34,993
A dietary permission becomes a lesson in self-restraint.

149
00:07:35,137 --> 00:07:38,513
This is Rashi's great gift: showing us that

150
00:07:38,553 --> 00:07:42,516
the Torah operates on multiple levels simultaneously.

151
00:07:42,756 --> 00:07:45,716
And third, there is a recurring thread about

152
00:07:45,796 --> 00:07:49,796
discernment, about the ability to see through appearances.

153
00:07:49,936 --> 00:07:52,916
Don't be fooled by the false prophet's miracle.

154
00:07:53,016 --> 00:07:55,556
Don't be swayed by a loved one's whispered

155
00:07:55,616 --> 00:07:56,596
enticement.

156
00:07:56,716 --> 00:07:59,800
The Torah, as Rashi reads it, is training

157
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:02,760
us not just in obedience but in wisdom,

158
00:08:02,813 --> 00:08:05,800
in the capacity to distinguish truth from deception

159
00:08:05,912 --> 00:08:11,254
even when deception wears a compelling disguise.Thank you

160
00:08:11,387 --> 00:08:13,654
so much for joining me on Rashi in

161
00:08:13,721 --> 00:08:14,134
Brief.

162
00:08:14,294 --> 00:08:17,574
Wishing you a week of clarity, wisdom, and

163
00:08:17,638 --> 00:08:20,294
good learning, and I'll see you next time.

